{
  "email": "james.oconnor@brightline.example",
  "reason": "Ticket sender reports warehouse office desktop not connecting to network; need account, devices, and usual hours."
}

{
  "hostname": "BL-DT-0108",
  "reason": "Check online state, last check-in, and warnings for the sender's registered desktop, which reportedly has no network connectivity."
}

{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi James, I checked your desktop (BL-DT-0108). It is showing offline, so it isn't reaching the network at all. Please try these steps: 1) Restart the desktop. 2) If it's on Wi-Fi, turn Wi-Fi off and on, or forget the network and reconnect. 3) If there's a network cable, make sure it's firmly plugged in at both ends. Then reply and let us know: Are other computers or phones in the warehouse office working on the network? Did the desktop come on normally, and does it show a network name or a 'no internet' message? If it's still offline after these steps, or if colleagues are also affected, we'll escalate it so it's sorted before your shift.",
  "internal_note": "Sender James O'Connor (user_id 8, Warehouse Supervisor, usual hours 06:00-14:00 CT). Account active, not blocked, no failed logins, VPN enabled, password set 2026-09-11. Only device: BL-DT-0108 (Windows 11 desktop). check_device_status: offline, last check-in 2026-10-11T01:37 UTC, disk 78% free, no warnings. Triage marked user_blocked true, but the account shows sign_in_blocked false; not acted on. Offline status confirms no network path (KB-014 quick check 3). Cause not yet isolated: could be office network, cable/Wi-Fi, or the device itself. Gave user KB-014 fix steps and asked whether colleagues are affected. Next: if user reports multiple people affected, escalate as office-wide network issue (P1/P2). If the device stays offline after restart and cable/Wi-Fi checks, escalate to Tier 2 for on-site check. No action tools were used; nothing queued.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-014"
  ],
  "confidence": "medium"
}
